	<key>NSHumanReadableCopyright</key>
	<string>© 2026 Paul F. Johnson. All rights reserved.</string>
	<!-- The upload scan reads the linked symbols, not what the code reaches, so
	     every privacy-guarded framework the binary links needs a purpose string.
	     The distribution build links the SDK assemblies only (see the comment on
	     MtouchLink in SuperSlapper.iOS.csproj), which drops the bindings nothing
	     references along with their frameworks, and these keys cover the ones that
	     survive. None of them is ever requested, so no permission prompt appears
	     at runtime.
	     HealthKit is deliberately gone. 1.0 build 7 was rejected for using the SMS
	     Fraud extension and HealthKit: the unlinked build carried HKHealthStore and
	     IdentityLookup whatever the game did. Trimming removed both, so the two
	     NSHealth* keys went with them - Apple asks for the plist keys to go as well
	     as the references, and a key left behind would keep claiming health access.
	     tools/release/check_ios_plist.py fails if either key or either framework
	     comes back.
	     NSUserTrackingUsageDescription is the exception: it is absent on purpose. The
	     build links AdSupport and AppTrackingTransparency and carries the
	     advertising-identifier selectors, but the game never reaches them and never
	     asks for tracking permission. App Store Connect reads the key itself as a
	     claim that the app tracks, and refuses to submit a binary carrying it unless
	     the App Privacy answers declare tracking, so a build that does not track must
	     not have it. tools/release/check_ios_plist.py checks that direction and fails
	     if the key is present. -->
	<key>NFCReaderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>SuperSlapper does not scan NFC tags.</string>
	<key>NSAppleMusicUsageDescription</key>
	<string>SuperSlapper does not access your Apple Music library.</string>
